From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter Laux <hunterlaux@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 01:37:47 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 03/74] MIPS: sc-mips: Wait for the secondary cache on Ingenic
 XBurst

On Ingenic XBurst CPUs a secondary cache operation is not complete when
its CACHE instruction retires: the write-backs it starts can still be on
their way to memory. Mainline's DMA cache maintenance then lets a stale
L2 line reach memory after the device has written there, or lets the CPU
read before the line is gone, and the CPU sees what the buffer held
before the transfer, with no error anywhere.

On the X2600 (XBurst II) this shows with the RTL8821CU USB Wi-Fi driver,
rtw88, whose register accesses are small control transfers to and from
a ring of 128 four-byte buffers: about one read in 500 returned the
value its buffer held 128 accesses earlier (counted against a record
of each buffer's last register and value, kept outside the buffers).
The stale reads hit only the buffers at the start of a 64-byte L2 line,
never those inside one, and they made rtw88 write wrong values back into
the chip (it reads, modifies and writes registers), failing its start-up
in some boots.

Ingenic's own kernels, for both XBurst generations, end their L2
operations with an uncached load (__fast_iob()), which waits for the
write-backs, and issue a SYNC before writing back. Do the same on XBurst.
With it the stale reads are gone: 0 in 30000 register reads over 6
boots, against about 10 in 5000 in every boot without it.
---
 arch/mips/mm/sc-mips.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/mips/mm/sc-mips.c b/arch/mips/mm/sc-mips.c
index 06ec304ad..e42061029 100644
--- a/arch/mips/mm/sc-mips.c
+++ b/arch/mips/mm/sc-mips.c
@@ -24,9 +24,24 @@
 /*
  * Writeback and invalidate the secondary cache before DMA.
  */
+/*
+ * On Ingenic XBurst CPUs a secondary cache operation is not done when its
+ * CACHE instruction retires: the write-backs it starts can still be on their
+ * way to memory when a DMA transfer, or the CPU, next accesses that memory.
+ * Ingenic's kernels wait for them with an uncached load, and so do we.
+ */
+static inline bool mips_sc_needs_drain(void)
+{
+	return current_cpu_type() == CPU_XBURST;
+}
+
 static void mips_sc_wback_inv(unsigned long addr, unsigned long size)
 {
+	if (mips_sc_needs_drain())
+		__sync();
 	blast_scache_range(addr, addr + size);
+	if (mips_sc_needs_drain())
+		__fast_iob();
 }
 
 /*
@@ -40,6 +55,8 @@ static void mips_sc_inv(unsigned long addr, unsigned long size)
 	cache_op(Hit_Writeback_Inv_SD, addr & almask);
 	cache_op(Hit_Writeback_Inv_SD, (addr + size - 1) & almask);
 	blast_inv_scache_range(addr, addr + size);
+	if (mips_sc_needs_drain())
+		__fast_iob();
 }
 
 static void mips_sc_enable(void)
